"use strict";(self.webpackChunkultimatelearning=self.webpackChunkultimatelearning||[]).push([[983],{364:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"topic":{"id":"data-types","title":"Data Types","overviewSimple":"Imagine you have different-sized jars to store things \u2014 a tiny jar for a single candy, a big jar for lots of candies, and a yes/no jar for simple answers. In Java, data types are like these jars \u2014 they decide what kind of information you can store.","overviewTechnical":["Java data types define the type of data a variable can hold, and how much memory it occupies.","Java has two main categories: Primitive data types and Non-Primitive (Reference) data types.","There are 8 primitive types: byte, short, int, long, float, double, char, and boolean.","Primitive types store actual values, while reference types store memory addresses pointing to objects.","byte (1 byte) is used for small, memory-efficient integer values.","short (2 bytes) is for slightly larger integers where memory usage matters.","int (4 bytes) is the default integer type used in most applications.","long (8 bytes) is used for large integer values and requires the \'L\' suffix for literals.","float (4 bytes) represents single-precision floating-point numbers with 6\u20137 digits of precision and uses the \'f\' suffix.","double (8 bytes) is double-precision floating-point with about 15 digits of precision and is the default for decimals.","char (2 bytes) stores a single Unicode character or number code.","boolean stores logical values true or false.","Reference types include Strings, Arrays, Classes, Interfaces, Enums, and Objects.","Default values: numeric types \u2192 0, boolean \u2192 false, char \u2192 \'\\\\u0000\', reference types \u2192 null.","Java supports implicit widening conversions (e.g., byte \u2192 int \u2192 long) and explicit narrowing conversions (e.g., double \u2192 int).","Wrapper classes exist for all primitive types to support object features, e.g., Integer, Double, Boolean."],"story":"Think of a magical toy box that has different sections \u2014 a tiny one for a bead, a medium one for marbles, and a large one for toy cars. Java uses different data types just like this toy box to organize different kinds of information.","realWorldAnalogy":"Data types are like containers in your kitchen \u2014 a spice bottle for little quantities, a jar for medium items, and a big box for rice. Each container can only hold a specific type of content.","keyPoints":["Java has 8 primitive types","Reference types store memory addresses","Default values exist for all types","Widening is automatic; narrowing requires casting","Wrapper classes provide object-based utilities","Memory size differs for each type","boolean only stores true/false","char stores Unicode characters"],"exampleSimple":"int age = 25;\\nboolean isJavaFun = true;\\nchar grade = \'A\';","exampleTechnical":["public class DataTypeDemo {\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        int count = 100;\\n        double price = 19.99;\\n        char letter = \'J\';\\n        boolean active = true;\\n\\n        System.out.println(\\"Count: \\" + count);\\n        System.out.println(\\"Price: \\" + price);\\n        System.out.println(\\"Letter: \\" + letter);\\n        System.out.println(\\"Active: \\" + active);\\n    }\\n}","public class TypeCastingExample {\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        int a = 10;\\n        double b = a;  // widening\\n\\n        double x = 9.7;\\n        int y = (int) x; // narrowing\\n\\n        System.out.println(\\"Widened: \\" + b);\\n        System.out.println(\\"Narrowed: \\" + y);\\n    }\\n}"],"extraNotes":["Primitive types are stored on the stack, while reference types point to objects on the heap.","String is a special reference type that behaves like a value type due to immutability.","Java\u2019s strict type framework helps catch errors early during compilation."],"bestPracticesSimple":["Pick the right type for what you store","Use meaningful variable names like age or price","Prefer int for whole numbers and double for decimals","Use boolean for true/false questions only"],"bestPracticesAdvanced":["Prefer using int instead of byte/short unless memory is a constraint","Use BigDecimal for precise financial calculations instead of double","Leverage wrapper types only when objects are required (e.g., collections)","Avoid unnecessary type casting to prevent data loss","Use var (Java 10+) for readability when the type is clear"]}}')}}]);